#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068779 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068619 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4640f978-9caa-5960-bec4-f153a1b4cc5d" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d86c6bd2-e528-55ed-bbfc-9f5ecbec024e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5924" :question/problem "Graph the function $y = -\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y = -\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/9d/9da0db2e6c97e507e82b7ca801e46802fabf91ad31ff53ad3cc9ae580151370a.png)\n\n- Translate it by one unit to the right to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x-1}.$ \n\n![](images/8c/8ce8c53f202f04b3b34a247b797f65f7616e8772afa1751de126ddd401417bd7.png)\n\nNotice that the vertical asymptote is now $x=1.$\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{2}{x-1}.$\n\n![](images/c9/c9b708a20d9c6c12f0016279e8d7a3ea55597e7645f0a9518f1c32e8a8406e47.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it by $1$ unit down to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1.$ \n\n![](images/e1/e100b552adb71cae1295492a58b0e60525813adb87d9815ba64a151a856f0c46.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote is now $y=-1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4640f978-9caa-5960-bec4-f153a1b4cc5d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050257}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562751 :question-generator/id #uuid "c63be814-5ae6-5d21-90c1-67df2ad8c11f" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186068779.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4640f978-9caa-5960-bec4-f153a1b4cc5d" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4675e39-a192-509f-84e6-821038b03fac" :answer/type {: